Information Highway Advisory Council
Summary of Meeting
February 22 and 23, 1995
Vancouver Trade and Convention Centre

ISSUE 8 -- (1st Reading) What controls, if any should be placed
on the information that is put on the network?

On behalf of the Access and Social Impacts Working Group, Neil
Baker presented the following recommendations for first reading:

It is recommended that:

1.  The federal government should implement a program to educate
the public, stakeholders and law enforcement officials that the
principles of law (currently embodied, for example, in the
Charter of Rights, the Criminal Code and the Human Rights Act)
also apply to computer mediated communication.

2.  The federal government should take immediate steps, in
conjunction with the provinces, owners, operators and users of
bulletin boards, Internet and Usenet sites and law enforcement
officials, to facilitate the development of a model code of
ethics and practices reflecting community standards and to
provide community awareness programs.

3.  In conjunction with the development of a model code of
conduct, the federal government should also encourage the
participants in the code of ethics exercise to develop
guidelines for complaint handling.

4.  The federal government should establish a technical
committee comprised of owners, operators and users of bulletin
boards, university system operators, Internet and Usenet sites
to identify technical solutions which ensure that only material
appropriate for a given audience can be accessed by that
audience. (For example, passwords help ensure restricted access;
user validation and certain payment mechanisms uphold age
restrictions; adaptive filters on home personal computers [PCs]
will screen out inappropriate violent or sexual content.)

5.  The federal government should actively pursue bilateral and
multilateral arrangements at the international level in order to
deal with jurisdictional problems in the control of offensive
communication on global networks.

Discussion:  The issue of what to do about offensive material is
one being faced today by university network administrators, and
a key question is whether Internet sites should be treated as
carriers or publishers. The AS&SI Working Group was asked to
address this legal question.

The Learning and Training Working Group proposed two additional
recommendations that would help deal with the problems educators
are now facing in attempting to protect young children from
offensive material (while ensuring that they gain the requisite
computer and network literacy skills).

