> >          age or disability.

> So, if I start a political activism mailing list I have a moral
> obligation to let those with opposing political goals on it?

A little context may be in order.  The above paragraph of Peter's is pretty
standard Australian anti-discrimination mumbo-jumbo, and is a must for
anything of a social nature here.  (Go fight it out with the Australian
politicians if you don't like it).  Mostly it is for employment and
shopkeeping purposes, not for speech purposes.

In the context of the Internet, if an Internet Service Provider was providing
access "for the public" it would have a duty not to discriminate against
applications for access based on race, religion, ethnic background, etc,
etc, etc.  If it did, it would find itself in court real fast.

On the other hand, if an ISP was set up for the express purpose of catering
to a particular group of people, then anti-discrimination laws would not
necessarily come into play.  For example, it would be silly for an ISP
catering to a particular ethnic community to be required to provide access
to every other community as well.  Many newspapers and magazines in Australia
operate on this basis, catering to particular ethnic groups.

This is like your example, since it is set up for a particular political
activism purpose.  If however you denied column space to political activists
of your variety on other grounds, such as age, race, religion, etc, you'd
get it in the neck real quick.

Anti-discrmination is a very tricky issue and it is not easy to give a
definite "yes or no" answer as to what is discrimination and what is not
discrimination.  The courts wrangle for years on such issues.

If anti-discrimination laws worry you on free speech grounds, then our
racial vilification laws would make your toes _really_ curl up.  Australia
doesn't have a firm free speech guarantee in our constitution.  I wish it
did, but constitutions are mighty hard things to change.  Especially when
those in power like things just the way they are.  Cest la vie!

In any case, the above is a _responsibility_, not a _requirement_.  People
should be responsible enough not to engage in such behaviour, just out of a
sense of "being a good citizen".  If they step out of line anyway, other
mechanisms then come into play.  Compare this with netiquette where there
is an expectation that users will act responsibly.  Most of the time they do.

I'm not going to argue the merits or demerits of Australian law on this
matter.  It just is.  Write to your local Australian consultate and protest
if it bothers you.  The long and the short of it is that if we want the
Australian powers that be to recognise the ERR we need to put in a few
media-grabbing things like supporting anti-discrimination.

> >          The responsibility to include adequate warning with any
> >          information that may mislead or endanger a naive reader.
> [...]

> So, I shouldn't posted the Bible without warning labels?

An extreme example.  Peter's clause is aimed at things like information
about explosives, drugs, etc.  The above comments about "Responsibility"
versus "requirement" apply.

Cheers,

Rhys.
