Perhaps there is a middle ground.

I think legal recourse should be the recourse of last resort against
bad speech. More speech, when practical, is the better recourse.

For example, in Britain, Mc Donalds restaurants are suing some
anti-meat protesters for telling alleged lies about Mc Donalds.  I
don't think Mc Donalds needs the courts; I think that instead of
bankrupting the protesters into total silence, Mc Donalds is perfectly
capable of defending itself with more speech.

On the Net, more speech is practical more often to more people than it
is in most other media.

This scheme does impose a responsibility. It means the "victims" of
defendable liable, have a responsibility to defend themselves and to
minimize damage. For example, if someone on the Net falsely says you
lied about something, it means that you have a responsibility try to
set the record straight.

