In the continuing saga of my case against the Central Intelligence Agency, 
I just received 
the document which follows.  The document is provided in its entirety 
(including some 
obvious errors).  I'll make a few comments at the end.


United States District Court
Southern District of Florida

Case No 94-1605-CIV-UNGARO_BENAGES

Kenneth C Stahl,
Plaintiff,

vs

R. James Woolsy, etc.
Defendant


Report and Recommendation

This cause if before the Court on the Defendant's Motion to
Dismiss (Docket Entry No. 5). This matter was referred to the
undersigned United States Magistrate Judge by the
Honorable Ursula Ungaro-Benages, United States District 
Judge for the Southern District of Florida and is now ripe for
judicial review.

Factual Background

This is an action pursuant to Title VII of the Civil Rights Act
of 1964, 42 U.S.C 2000e alleging unlawful discrimination due
to sex and race.  Plaintiff, a white male, alleges he was 
unlawfully discriminated against by the Central Intelligence
Agency (the "CIA") during the period between 1989 to July 
1990, in that he was removed from his position and replaced
by a less qualified white female. Upon protesting the action,
he was reassigned to a less responsible position.

Plaintiff was advised to file a grievance with the Agency and
alleges he was not aware or advised that he could or should
contact an EEO officer.  Accordingly, Plaintiff did not contact an
EEO counsel until January 13, 1992. Plaintiff voluntarily
resigned from the CIA in May, 1992 and filed a formal
complaint on April 26, 1993.

Plaintiff's Complaint was dismissed and he was notified of the
action on June 18, 1993. On July 7, 1993 Plaintiff appealed the
agency decision. The Equal Opportunity Commission affirmed
the Agency dismissal on September 14, 1993. That notification 
was received by Plaintiff on September 17, 1993 along with the
notification of his right to sue.

Plaintiff timely filed this action on October 18, 1993, in the
Middle District of North Carolina. That action has been transferred
to the Southern District of Florida.

Standard of Review

Defendants predicate their request for dismissal of the Complain
on Rule 12(b)(6) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. When a
federal court considers a 12(b)(6) motion of dismissal at the
pleading stage, the court must apply the Federal Rules of Civil
Procedure and not state the law in question. Federal Deposit Ins
Corp v Haddan, 778 F Supp. 197 (S.D.N.Y. 1987); Hiatt v
Schreibber, 599 F Supp 1142 (D.C. Colo 1984). Pursuant to Rule
8(a) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, a complaint shall
contain "a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the
pleader is entitled to relief." Caster v Hennessay, 781 F. 2d 1569,
1570 (11th Cir 1986). Courts have interpreted this standard
liberally. Whilte the pleading must be sufficient to give the
defendant fair notice of what the claim is and the grounds upon
which it rests, the pleading is not required to set forth the detailed
facts upon which the claim is based. Conley. v. Gibson 355 U.S.
41, 47 (1957). The parties may, through discovery, inquire further
into the details underlying the claim. Bazal v Belford Trucking
co., Inc 442 F Supp 1089, 1102 (S.D. Fla 1977). The Eleventh
Circuit has recognized "the well-established policy in the federal
courts favoring liberal pleading requirements." Warner V.
Alexander Grand & Co. 828 F.2d 1528, 1531 (11th Cir. 1987).

As to the viability of a cause of action the court must accept all
well-pleaded allegations of complaint as true, and all ambiguities
or doubts concerning sufficiency of the claim must be resolved
in favor of the pleader. Hishon v. King & Spalding, 467 U.S. 69,
73 (1984); Stevens v Department of Health and Human Services,
901 F.2d 1571, 1573 (11th Cir 1990); Parr v Woodmen of World
Life Insurance Co 791 F.2d 888, 889-890 (11th Cir 1986).
Consideration of matters beyond the four corners of the
Complaint is improper. Milburn v United States, 734 F.2d 762
(11th Cir 1984); Thomas v. Burlington Industries, Inc. 769 F.
Supp 368, 370 (S.D. Fla 1991). Furthermore, the court cannot
dismiss unless it appears beyond doubt that under no set of
facts can plaintiff state a claim which would entitle it to relief.
Bradberry v. Pinellas County, 789 F.2d 1513, 1515 (11th Cir.
1986); In re Asbestos Litigation, 679 F. Supp. 1096, 1098
(S.D. Fla 1987); Donovan v American Leader Newspapers, Inc.
524 F. Supp. 1144 (M.D. Fla. 1981). "The issue is not whether
a plaintiff will ultimately prevail but whether the claimant is
entitled to offer evidence to support the claims." Scheuer v
Rhodes, 416 U.S. 232, 236 (1974); In re Asbestos Litigation,
679 F. Supp at 1098.

Legal Analysis

Defendant raises two challenges to the Complaint. First,
Defendant argues that Plaintiff's Complaint should be
dismissed for failure to exhaust administrative remedies.
Second, Defendant maintains the Complaint fails to state a
claim upon which relief can be grated because of failure to
plead facts sufficient to find a prima facie violation of Title
VII. Each of these charges shall be addressed in turn.

Exhaustion of Administrative Remedies

Plaintiff moves for dismissal of the Complaint on grounds that
Defendant has failed to exhaust his administrative remedies.
Plaintiff was denied agency relief due to the alleged untimely
filing of his discrimination allegations.  The Agency decision
found that Plaintiff failed to seek EEG counseling in a timely
fashion.  EEOC Regulations 29 C.F.R 1613.214(a)(1)(i) in
effect at the time of the alleged discriminatory action required
that complaints of discrimination be brought to the attention of
the EEG counselor within thirty (30) calendar days of the
alleged discriminatory event, the effect date of an alleged
discriminatory personnel action, or the date that the aggrieved
person knew or reasonably should have known of the
discriminatory event or personnel action.

Plaintiff concedes that he failed to contact an EEG counselor
within the thirty day time period, but argues same does not
act as a bar to his action in that, under the circumstances, the
thirty day time limitation was equitably tolled.  This Court agrees.

Compliance with the thirty day time limit is not a prerequisite
to the District Court's jurisdiction, but rather, is similar to a
statutory limitation which is subject to equitable tolling.
Miranda v. B&B Cash Grocery Store, Inc, 975 F.2d 1578, 1531
(11th Cir. 1992); Washington, V Ball, 890 F.2d413,414 (11th
Cir 1989); Bates v Tennessee Valley Authority, 851 F.2d 1366,
1368 (11th Cir. 1988). "The statute may be equitably tolled in
the following situations: (1) when a state court action is
pending; (2) when the Defendant has concealed an act
supporting the Title VII cause of action; and (3) when the
Defendant had misled the employee regarding the nature of his
rights under Title VII." Washington, 890 F.2d at 414 (citing
Manning v Carlin, 786 F.2d 1108, 1109 (11th Cir. 1986). The
three situations outlined above, however, are not exhaustive
Id at 415.

In the case sub judice, Plaintiff alleges that the complaint
procedure was not readily available, but existed in a locked
safe in a controlled situation. Plaintiff's Compl. 8. Viewing this
allegation as true, as we must, this Court cannot find that under
no set of facts can it state a claim which would entitle him  to
relief. Bradberry v Pinellas County, 789 F.2d 1513, 1515 (11th
Cir 1986). Once again, "[the issue is not whether a plaintiff will
ultimately prevail, but whether the claimant is entitled to offer
evidence to support the claims." Schever v Rhodes 416 U.S.
232, 236 (1974).

Here, Plaintiff's allegation  that the complaint procedure was
not readily available, but existed in a locked safe in a
controlled situation, fits neatly into category umber three
referenced in Bates, to wit: When the Defendant has mislead
the employee regarding the nature of his rights under Title
VII. See also Kanzanzas v Walt Disney World Co., 704 F.2d
1527, 1530 (11th Cir) (employers failure to post EEOC
notices informing claimants off their rights and obligations
results in equitable tolling of the time period in which an
employee may file a charge of discrimination until such time
as the employer either retains an attorney or acquires general
knowledge of rights), cent denied 464 U.S. 982 (1983) Miranda
v. B&B Cash Grocery Store, Inc., 975 F.2d 15618, 1532 (11th
Cir 1992) (equitable tolling of limitations period for female to
pursue Civil Rights claim for gender-based wage
discrimination was warranted where employee had been led by
employer to believe that unfair treatment would be rectified);
Washington v Ball, 890 F.2d 413, Title VII action was tolled
when employee was misled by court regarding nature of his
rights.

Sufficiency of Complaint Allegations

Defendant moves for dismissal of the Complaint on grounds
that Plaintiff has failed to plead facts which would establish
a prima facie case of discrimination under Title VII. This
Court disagrees.

The Complaint charged discrimination by Defendant on the
basis of Plaintiff's race and sex by "removing Plaintiff from
his position and demoting, transferring and denying Plaintiff
advancement opportunities." Compl 10. The Complaiknt goes
on to allege that Plaintiff is a white male, that he was removed
from his position in July 1990 and replaced by a less qualified
female and that he was subjected to a punitive assignment
and given less responsibility as a result of his protestations.
Compl 6. These allegations, taken as true, are sufficient to state
a claim for Title VII discrimination. McDonnel Douglas  Corp. v.
Green 411 U.S. 792 (1973).

In accordance with the above and foregoing, it is hereby 

RECOMMENDED  that the Defendant's Motion to Dismiss be DENIED.

The parties have ten (10) days from the date of this Report and
Recommendation within which to serve and file written objections,
if any with the Honorable Ursala Ungaro-Benages, United States
District Judge. Failure to file objections timely shall bar the parties
from attacking on appeal the factual findings contained herein.
LoConte v Dugger, 847 F.2d 745 (11th Cir 1988), cent denied, 488
U.S. 958 (1988); RTC v Hllmark Builders, Inc 996 F.2d 1144,
1149 (11th Cir 1993).

RESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED this March 25, 1995, in Chambers.

LINNEA R. JOHNSON
UNITED STATES MAGISTRATE JUDGE


