If the answer is no, then the DCI should seriously think about 
firing their lawyers and get some that are reasonably copetant.
Oh.  I forgot.  Lawyers are no different from any other government
employee.  They are paid on the GS scale.  That being the case,
the government cannot afford to pay lawyers the same
compensation as if they worked for a private law firm.  I'll leave
you, the reader, to draw your own conclusions about that.

So, now the CIA must start taking this case seriously.  It has 
been before the court for over a year and a half now and I bet
that in all that time they have never conducted a thorough 
investigation of the facts because they felt that the case would
never get this far.  It is also complicated by a tradition within
the CIA that it is generally permissible to prevaricate to anyone
conducting an investigation if the truth would tend to implicate
anyone within the CIA of any wrongdoing.  How do I know this
to be true?  At one point an official I was dealing with at the
CIA told me the results of a conversation with the Director of
the Office of Communication.  When I pointed out that a specific
bit of information was factually incorrect, the individual replied
that  it is within managerial perogitive to lie.

I doubt if the CIA even knows at this point the names of all
individuals who were involved and whose input eventually
cause the discriminatory action against me.  Probably the only
person they have ever talked to is the D/OC and his response
was probably that he and his office had done nothing wrong 
and therefore there is nothing to investigate and would then
go on to say that since he, alone, is responsible for all that 
happens in OC, that it would be totally unnecessary to question
anyone else.  Any you know what?  Given the culture at the 
CIA, the investigator probably nodded his head and agreed - 
after all, the D/CO is an SIS level officer, and at that level
they are treated like gods.

I'd like to put out an invitation to everyone out there in
the cyberworld.  If you were ever an employee of the CIA,
especially if you were a part of OC, and you were the victim
of discrimination or other grievable action, I'd like to hear
from you.  If you would be willing to write letters that detail
the treatment you received, that would be even better.  If
you would be willing to testify that the basic culture of the
Agency pre-supposed managerial rights to make decisions
regardless of their legality, that would be outstanding.
There are just countless ways that you could be very helpful.
Write letters to the president, the attorney general,
congressmen, the Federal District Court in Miami.  Post 
messges on the internet.  So far this has been a lonely
fight for me and even though I will pursue this to the 
end without support from others, it would be nice to know
that there are others out there who have had their lives
ruined by the CIA and who are willing to talk about it openly.

I will close by saying something I've said on several other
occasions but which I feel the need to repeat once more.
I have no beef with the overall mission of the CIA.  It has
and does serve an important function in the formulation and
enactment of our foreign policy.  My complaint is against
the personnel policies in the agency which tend to
discriminate against individuals who are not political
cronies of managers.  I also firmly believe that the CIA 
should not be exempt from public scrutiny in the way that
personnel policies are used to terrorize employees and
take away their rights as citizens all in the name of
"needs of the service".  I further firmly believe that the
CIA should be fully complient with all OPM rules and
regulations and that they must be accountable for their 
actions and be denied the right to claim that personnel
actions are strictly an internal matter which are not
subject to review or reversal.

Thanks for your time.


