djones@insight.mcmaster.ca (David Jones) writes:
>Therefore, the so-called "responsibilities" are simply a list of suggestions
>about how people should be nice to each other.
>
>Although you claim these are not "requirements", I suspect that
>many people will be eager to "enforce" this list.

Many people are already eager to do so _and_are_doing_so_. The whole point 
of the responsibilities list is to promote a standard by which people who
would legislate these responsibilities can be properly described as acting 
unethically and against the better interests of all Internet participants.

How can such a standard be constructed and understood without such explicit 
statements?

>Consider the unfortunate position that EFA will be in after the following:

>	John Smith has his Internet account suspended because
>	he quoted a passage from the Bible, without any warning.
>	[1.4 -> advisory labels on ALL communications]

>	John Smith posts this passage in a newsgroup discussing Budhism.
>	[1.1 -> (arguably) not relevant to newsgroup]

Certainly these two are described as unethical by the ERR. But should you
attempt to prevent John Smith's actions by law? You should not, because
such laws cannot be framed without affecting people who are engaged in
quite ethical behaviour. 

However, happily, John Smith's actions are easily and commonly dealt with 
through non-legislative mechanisms. Friendly advice, flames and kill-files
are all common and effective resorts when faced with such behaviour, and
greatly preferable to any police action.

>	John Smith is an educator and provides access to this
>	religious material in school, even to student of other religions
>	[1.2 -> responsibility to restrict access to information for minors]

Note that I don't speak for EFA, and EFA has not yet ratified
this document. However, so far as EFA might be concerned, I hope that what
parents show their children and what educators teach in schools should
be a matter for those parents and educators to determine. There are
many mechanisms whereby educators are held accountable to parents, and
I don't think it is EFA's business to provide such mechanisms.

Why is this? If we attempt to recommend what educators may and may not teach,
and what parents may or may not allow then we lose any claim to impartiality,
because such recommendations can only reveal our own cultural biases. In order
to be impartial we have to cop out some place, and this is probably the place.

>Parents and administrators can cite all kinds of "violations" of
>the EFA guidelines.  Where will EFA be then?  How will they be able
>to defend his position?

So long as the ERR fairly represents the views of the EFA, the EFA will be
able to use it to suggest which "violations" are properly the concern of
legislators, and which are properly the concern of individuals, and in this
case which are properly the concern of parents and educators. This seems to
be a pretty consistent line to take, and one that may be considerably more
effective than just standing around bitching every time some blue-nose 
feels the need to oppress his neighbours.

>I think this wishy-washy list of pseudo-responsibilities is wrong-headed.

You're welcome to your opinion. I invite you to support it so that the document
can be improved by its light.

>I think the EFA should make a clear distinction among three related aspects
>of "responsibility":

>	liability
>[...]
>	contractual obligations
>[...]
>	etiquette
>[...]

I'm curious as to how you think that this split up would improve the
effectiveness of the document.  I'm also curious to see a list of
responsibilities drawn up along these lines; if you would care to do so
then it would provide an excellent basis for criticism and improvement
of whatever position EFA eventually takes, as well as a good
alternative for people who don't care for the ERR. I'd really like you
to take the time to do this - though be warned that it's not as easy as
you might imagine.

