Hello all,

 I have recently been brought into Judicial Affairs at the Rochester 
Institute of Technology (a private university) on charges of harrassment.
Here's what happened...I have been having problems with another student
ever since last spring, when a discussion in a VMS notes conference on the
RIT computer system erupted in a flame war.  Around that time, I received
a journal file, written by her, through e-mail (she had e-mailed it to
a friend of mine--not to mention at least 5 others and possibly more--who
forwarded it to me). 
   Recently, this student posted in her .plan file a story that is
quite clearly an attack on a friend of mine (though she disguised it
as a fairy tale called "The Beast").  I replied by taking her journal
file, rotating the letters (not rot-13, but close), and posting it
in my .plan file. She went to her advisor (who happens to work for
Judicial Affairs at RIT), and I got called into Judicial Affairs for
an "administrative hearing." Besides this, all my computer accounts were
disabled over a weekend before I was notified of any of this.
   At the hearing, the person who handled it basically said I was 
automatically guilty, that I had no right to own the journal file, and
that I had to explain why I even still had the file.  She said it was 
"private business" and that I had no right to have it (mostly because
the student who had complained didn't _want_ me to own it).  I asked
why, if it were so private, had she mailed it to all her friends?  I
got no answer other than that it was completely irrelevant and that
my owning it and making it known that I owned it were harrassment.
Incidentally, I was also told that I was being childish and that I 
was wasting everyone's time. It was heavily implied that I should
take the rap for this, take the punishment, and not waste Judicial
Affairs' time by appealing. 
   After the meeting I talked to someone in the Ombudsman's office and
decided to appeal.  I also got in contact with Mike Godwin, who told
me that I should treat this as if I were going to court and to get
myself a lawyer (also incidentally, I was given conflicting information
on whether I was allowed to bring a lawyer to my hearing...the top sheet
said I could only bring an RIT student, staff, or faculty advisor who
is _not_ a lawyer, but the sheet I had to sign stated that I had the
right to have a lawyer present before answering any questions).
The next day I talked to a lawyer who works for RIT, and he told me he
didn't even see the issue here, since she had not copyrighted the journal,
hadn't reserved any rights, hadn't told anyone _not_ to distribute the file,
and had e-mailed it. He gave me the name of a lawyer in town who I might
contact (the RIT lawyer can't, obviously, represent me against RIT).
   Yes, I know this whole thing is ridiculous and that if I were smart
I would have dropped the whole thing ages ago.  But I don't think that
I should be punished for what has been a lot of foolishness on many people's
part.
  
   Anyway, now that I've bored you all to tears :) what is your opinion?
Was it harrassment?  Did I _not_ have the right to own the file? Any further
advice? 


