In article <pete.797391777@extro> Peter Merel <pete@extro.su.oz.au> writes:
>          The right of access to any publicly available information,
>          restricted only by financial constraints.

Everything after the first comma is irrelevant IMHO. Financial constraints are 
a constant.

>          The right to control and license intellectual property invested
>          in original expressions, but not in algorithms nor other
>          abstract specifications.

It is dangerous to make rules that may conflict with existing law. Like it or 
not, ths above does not reflect the current state of law in many 
jurisdictions. If you left out everything after and including "invested" no 
conflict would exist. The struggle to make the rest of the sentence not 
conflict is another battle. On the whole, I find this right rather irrelevant.

>          The right to receive any information from any source.

I find this unclear - it is too easy to interpret it as a right to demand that 
someone else transmits the desired information. Perhaps something like "the 
right to receive information transmitted from any source"?

>          The right of every person to restrict access by any means to
>          any information that they originate or receive.

"By any means" is a red rag to the legal bull. I'd drop that phrase. Is 
murder, for example, an acceptable means to prevent a person accessing 
something?

>          The right to ignore information of any nature provided that
>          this ignorance does not infringe upon the rights of others.

How can my ignoring information possibly infringe on the rights of others? A 
genuine question :-)

>          The right to seek legal recourse for damages caused by the
>          actions and expressions of others.

Seems redundant - this depends on, and is well codified in, existing law.

>          The right, as administrator of a system or as moderator of a
>          moderated forum, to deny participation in that system or forum
>          to any person for any reason.

This may well run foul of anti-discrimination law. Needs much more thought, I 
think!

>          The responsibility to neither harrass nor vilify others on the
>          basis of race, religion, ethnic background, nationality,
>          gender, political affiliation, sexual preference, age or
>          disability.

It seems simpler to drop everything after "others". Don't you think?

>          The responsibility to represent yourself, your observations and
>          opinions, and the expressions of others sincerely and without
>          misrepresentation.

Why not just replace everything after "sincerely" with "accurately"?

>          The responsibility to observe the rights and responsibilities
>          above, and to educate others about them.

I don't like the bit about educating others. There are too many unpleasantly 
evangelist religions about for me to accept that it is anyone's duty to 
educate except where the potential student is seeking and requesting 
education.

In general, a good effort! My last (nitpicky) point is that some of the righst 
start with "the right of every person" and some start with "the right" - I  
think the latter is better.

Regards, K.


