kadie@sal.cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:

>Peter Merel <pete@extro.su.oz.au> writes:

>>          The right, as administrator of a system or as moderator of a
>>          moderated forum, to deny participation in that system or forum
>>          to any person for any reason.

>This says, for example, that a university sys admin, can expell a
>student from the univeristy computer without cause, due process, or
>appeal. I hope that no universities give their sys admin (or anyone
>else) such authority.

If a system administrator oversteps his authority then you can simply
go over his head; if a university administration approves his actions
then you are plumb out of luck. I don't see how this document can help
such a situation.

>I think it needs something like

>          ... to any person for any legal reason.

Whose laws should this document uphold? I dare say that a legal reason in
some countries might be "because he made a joke about the prophet and now 
he must die". 

Again, this document is an ethical position, not a legal position; if it
takes a stand on legalities then it becomes culturally biased, and if it
becomes culturally biased then I think its purpose is defeated.

>or

>          ... to any person for any reason consistent with the system's
>          or forum's legal obligations and procedures.

Opens up a similar can of worms. One good reason for leaving it as it is
is to warn against legal procedures; imagine, for example, that you have
a Canter&Siegel account on your machine, and suddenly all your connectivity
is destroyed by incoming mail-bombs. They haven't done any spamming - they
just have an account there that identifies them as C&S. What legal reason
do you have for removing their account?

If you are legally required to accept and maintain their account, then they 
can destroy any ISP business that they choose simply by investing $50 in
a shell acount. I don't trust lawyers to do right by service providers and
moderators; I trust freedom-of-choice to do that. If a moderator or a 
service-provider has policies that exclude you, there are many other avenues
that you can use to maintain your concerns, and it is very likely that
moderators and service providers that abuse their powers in this way won't
last long. If that's the case, then why bring the lawyers into it?

